just as President Trump is pushing for a  massive increase in the Pentagon's  budget a new audit says the Defense  Department has lost hundreds of millions  of dollars national security  correspondent Jennifer Griffin has the  latest tonight from the Pentagon the  Pentagon can't account for 800 million  dollars that according to a shocking  internal audit of the Defense Logistics  Agency done by Ernst &amp; Young the  Department of Defense continues to have  unresolved accounting issues and is  unable to provide sufficient evidential  support for complete and accurate  financial statements on a timely basis  raising questions about how the Pentagon  is managing taxpayer money the White  House plans to ask for billions more for  defense on top of its current 700  billion dollar budget the largest in the  federal bureaucracy  last year the Pentagon comptroller  ordered an audit for the first time ever  it's been clear to me for 25 years they  never followed the money everybody  respects the military and we ought to  they put their lives on the line for our  freedom and liberties we ought to but I  think they get too much leeway just  because of that respect Ernst &amp; Young  found that the Defense Logistics Agency  which oversees Pentagon purchasing and  has been compared to Walmart failed to  document eight hundred million dollars  in construction projects DLA employs 25  thousand people and processes 100,000  orders a day a spokesperson for the  agency responded quote DLA concurs with  Ernst &amp; Young's assessment of our  failure to properly account for and  track funding to specific construction  projects while there were shortcomings  in documentation there was no loss of  accountability of real property or  associated funding some experts are  willing to give DLA the benefit of the  doubt if you look back at some of the IT  systems that DoD uses for its accounting  and financial systems some of them date  back to you know the 1960s 1970s the  past 10 years defense secretary after  defense secretary has tried to begin a  complete audit of the Pentagon by law  they were supposed to complete one by  December 3rd  first now we are learning the cost of  such an audit could top more than a  billion dollars Brett Jumoke tell us  about US troop drawdown in Iraq  well Pentagon officials tell us hundreds  of US troops have started to leave Iraq  but about seven thousand US troops  remain on the ground there to Train  local forces most of American troops  will return to the US but some are  headed to Afghanistan among them drone  units that provide badly needed  intelligence and surveillance Iran has  long pressured Iraq to rid itself of US  troops this announcement follows Iraq's  declaration in December that the fight  against Isis is over and precedes an  election in Iraq in May Brett Jennifer  Griffin live at the Pentagon Jennifer  thank you
 

